# The front door for a stack behind a TLS terminator it does not own — `tailscale serve`, a reverse
# proxy you already run, a corporate load balancer. Mounted at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile in place of the
# sibling Caddyfile when OHMAIL_EXTERNAL_TLS is set; compose composes the mount path from it.
#
# Plain HTTP on port 80, no certificate of its own and no ACME order. OHMAIL_ORIGIN keeps naming the
# https address the terminator presents — the cookie host, the passkey identity and every link the
# app writes come from it. Point the terminator at port 80 on OHMAIL_BIND. Port 443 has nothing
# behind it here.
#
# NO Strict-Transport-Security here, deliberately. This door holds no certificate, so it cannot
# know whether the one your terminator presents is publicly trusted — and a browser that has
# stored HSTS for a host will not let anyone click past a certificate warning on it. Set the
# header at the terminator, which is the half that knows: max-age=63072000.
#
# X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto are honoured from OHMAIL_TLS_TERMINATOR and nowhere else,
# by this proxy and, for the visitor's address, by the api behind it (compose hands it the same
# range). Under Docker a same-box terminator arrives from the compose network's gateway, never
# from loopback, so the default is Docker's own address pool; the terminator must then set or
# replace X-Forwarded-For, or a visitor names its own address. For a relay that adds nothing
# (socat, stunnel, a TCP balancer) set OHMAIL_TLS_TERMINATOR=0.0.0.0/32, which no peer can be.
{
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static {$OHMAIL_TLS_TERMINATOR:172.16.0.0/12}
	}
}

http://:80 {
	import /etc/caddy/routes.caddy
}
